## Proof

**Claim.** In the category **G-Set** of left $G$-sets and $G$-equivariant maps, every epimorphism is surjective (and conversely).

We use the cokernel-pair characterization of epimorphisms, valid in any category with pushouts:

> A morphism $f:X\to Y$ is epi iff the two coprojections $\iota_1,\iota_2:Y\to Y\amalg_X Y$ into the pushout of $f$ with itself are equal.

(Reason: $\iota_1 f=\iota_2 f$ always; if $f$ is epi this forces $\iota_1=\iota_2$. Conversely, if $\iota_1=\iota_2$, then any $g,h:Y\to Z$ with $gf=hf$ factor uniquely through the pushout as $g=u\iota_1$, $h=u\iota_2$, hence $g=h$.)

**G-Set has pushouts.** Indeed $G\text{-}\mathbf{Set}\cong \mathbf{Set}^{G}$ is a presheaf category, so colimits are computed pointwise; equivalently the forgetful functor $U:G\text{-}\mathbf{Set}\to\mathbf{Set}$ creates colimits. Thus the pushout of $f:X\to Y$ with itself is the set-theoretic pushout $Y\amalg_X Y$ equipped with the induced $G$-action.

**Compute the pushout.** As a set,
$$Y\amalg_X Y \;=\; (Y\sqcup Y)\big/\!\sim,\qquad \iota_1(f(x))\sim \iota_2(f(x))\ \text{for all }x\in X.$$
The relation $\sim$ is generated by these single-step identifications; since both copies are identical and the only identifications are across the two copies at points of $\operatorname{im}(f)$, we have
$$\iota_1(y)=\iota_2(y)\ \text{in }Y\amalg_X Y \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad y\in\operatorname{im}(f).$$
(The $G$-action preserves $\operatorname{im}(f)$ because $f$ is equivariant, so the identifications are $G$-stable and no extra points get identified.)

**Conclusion.** The two coprojections $\iota_1,\iota_2:Y\to Y\amalg_X Y$ are equal as maps iff $\iota_1(y)=\iota_2(y)$ for every $y\in Y$, iff $\operatorname{im}(f)=Y$, iff $f$ is surjective. By the cokernel-pair characterization, $f$ is epi iff $\iota_1=\iota_2$. Therefore

$$f\text{ is an epimorphism in }G\text{-}\mathbf{Set}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ f\text{ is surjective}.$$

In particular, **yes**: epimorphisms in $G\text{-}\mathbf{Set}$ are necessarily surjective.

### PROOF COMPLETE
